<b>יא.</b> "May it be Your will." After chatat one should not say this, since chatat is not voluntary and comes only for an inadvertent sin whose intentional violation incurs karet (Tur). Beit Yosef implies this should not be said even after asham talui, following the Sages that it is not a voluntary offering except in specific doubt-cases (see YD siman 5). Tur, however, allows saying it after asham. Explanations were offered (from nezirut) that asham can arise from voluntary commitment. Magen Avraham challenges these proofs and notes many difficulties. His practical ruling: after asham one may say a conditional prayer, "If I am liable for asham..." since asham does not require prior knowledge like chatat; after chatat, say nothing. On Shabbat and Yom Tov, do not say "Yehi ratzon" because voluntary offerings are not brought then, though reciting the passages as Torah study is fine. If one is a Torah scholar, better to learn relevant sugyot instead.
